S5-07 ELF stack-copy invocation counters — implementation slice — 2026-11-17

Purpose
-------
Add one opt-in bounded observation for the exact initial-stack platform-string
copy identified by the schema-v13 PF event history and schema-v14 pre-copy
snapshot. The observation measures where the source pair's +1 `cr3_changes`
appears relative to that invocation. It does not alter guest semantics,
strict comparison, or the optional panic-frame allowlist.

Capture contract
----------------
The existing `--execution-observations-elf-stack-copy` option now starts one
invocation capture only after the existing register/RIP gate, verified direct
CALL bytes `e8 47 d9 34 00` at `0xffffffff81999bd4`, and exact `x86_64\0`
source bytes. The return boundary is the checked next RIP
`0xffffffff81999bd9`; it is not read from the pre-CALL stack. The specialized
instruction path remains active through that return boundary, including
quantum boundaries, then stops collecting the invocation.

Schema v16 records cumulative entry, return, and run-stop snapshots for CR3,
delivered PF/exceptions, `cr3_changes`, CPL0 RDTSC reads, and retired
instructions through the current boundary. Inside the invocation it records
actual `Cr3Write` events (the same event kind counted by `stop.cr3_changes`),
delivered #PF events, and an RDTSC-by-RIP histogram. Capacity is one
invocation, 64 CR3 events, 64 PF events, 64 RDTSC sites, and 1,000,000 retired
instructions. Overflow and run-end-before-return are explicit statuses with
omitted counts; the first observed return boundary is retained.

Strict loading checks the exact return RIP, fixed cap, counter monotonicity,
boundary/event retired ordering, retained CR3 chain, checked RDTSC sums, and
reconciliation of the final nested snapshot with the real `runner.stop`
object. Schema v7–v15 paths remain supported. A gate mismatch is preserved as
a loadable diagnostic status without inventing invocation boundaries.

